    84	## 2. Statements and proofs
    85	
    86	### C1. Diagonal reduction
    87	
    88	*Claim.* If `(F, G)(x, y, t) = (F̃, G̃)(x + y, t)` solves (2), then `(F̃, G̃)(x, t)` solves (1). Consequently,
    89	if (2) passes the generic N-soliton test for a type pattern, so does (1).
    90	
    91	*Proof.* For functions of `s = x + y` and t, `∂_y = ∂_x = ∂_s`, so `D_y P(D_x, D_t)F·G = D_x P(D_x, D_t)F·G`,
    92	both evaluated at `s`; the first equations coincide. For the test: the seed conditions do not involve M
    93	(for `M(κ) ≠ 0`), so seeds of (2) with `l_i = k_i` are seeds of (1), and the recursion and residuals of (2) at
    94	`l_i = k_i` are literally those of (1) (the multiplier `λ_μ − λ_ν` becomes `k_μ − k_ν`). The residuals of (2)
    95	are zero rational functions; the specialisation `l = k` keeps the determinants `−2 k_S P(κ_S)P(−κ_S)`
    96	nonzero polynomials, so the residuals of (1) vanish identically. ∎
    97	
    98	### C2. The F = G sector
    99	
   100	*Claim.* Let P be odd and `M ∈ {D_x, D_y}`. With seeds `a_i = b_i`, the recursion gives `F_S = G_S` for all S,
   101	and these coefficients are those of the single-function recursion for `MP f·f = 0`. For N = 3 the
   102	single-function recursion passes iff Hirota's product ansatz passes.
   103	
   104	*Proof.* Induction on |S|. If `F_T = G_T` for `|T| < |S|`, the first equation at `n = 1_S` reads
   105	`P(κ_S)(F_S − G_S) + Σ_{T≠∅,S} F_T F_{S∖T} P(κ_T − κ_{S∖T}) = 0`; the sum vanishes because the terms `T` and
   106	`S∖T` cancel (P odd), so `F_S = G_S` (as `P(κ_S) ≠ 0`). The second equation is then the single-function
   107	equation for `B = MP`. For N = 3 the single-function residual at `n = (2,1,1)` equals
   108	`2[f_1 f_123 B(κ_2+κ_3) + f_12 f_13 B(κ_2−κ_3)]`, which vanishes iff `f_123 = A_12A_13A_23 f_1f_2f_3`
   109	(given `f_1 B(κ_2+κ_3) ≠ 0`), i.e. iff the product ansatz satisfies the `(1,1,1)` equation, which is
   110	Hirota's condition; for the product ansatz all coefficients with an entry 2 vanish automatically. ∎
   111	
   112	So if `MP f·f = 0` fails the 3ST, the two-function system fails the 3ST (at `a_i = b_i`, hence generically).
