   143	a seed with `l_3 = 0` is in any case excluded by the seed definition in §0.)
   144	
   145	*Proof.* Level 12: the lower terms `F_1G_2`, `F_2G_1` vanish, so `F_12 = G_12 = 0`. Level 13 (only `F_1G_3`
   146	contributes): `F_13P(κ_13) + G_13P(−κ_13) = −a_1b_3P(κ_1−κ_3)` and
   147	`m_13[F_13P(κ_13) − G_13P(−κ_13)] = −a_1b_3 (m_1−m_3) P(κ_1−κ_3)`; subtracting and using
   148	`m_13 − (m_1 − m_3) = 2m_3` gives `G_13 P(−κ_13) = −a_1b_3 P(κ_1−κ_3) m_3/m_13`. Likewise
   149	`G_23P(−κ_23) = −a_2b_3P(κ_2−κ_3)m_3/m_23`.
   150	Level 123: only `T = 1` (`a_1G_23`) and `T = 2` (`a_2G_13`) contribute besides `F_123, G_123`. With
   151	`X = a_1G_23P(κ_1−κ_2−κ_3)`, `Y = a_2G_13P(κ_2−κ_1−κ_3)` the two equations give
   152	`G_123P(−κ_123) = −[X m_23 + Y m_13]/m_123`, i.e.
   153	`G_123 = a_1a_2b_3 m_3 Φ / (m_123 P(−κ_123)P(−κ_13)P(−κ_23))`. At `n = (2,1,1)` the pairs are
   154	`(123, 1)` (contains `G_1 = 0`), `(12, 13)`, `(13, 12)` (contain `F_12 = G_12 = 0`) and `(1, 123)`, so the
   155	coefficient is `a_1G_123P(−κ_23)`. ∎
   156	
   157	`two_function_3ss.py`, Part B, re-derives this identity with every value `P(Σ ε_iκ_i)` an independent symbol
   158	(only the dispersion relations imposed) and arbitrary `m_i`: printed `closed form ... True`.
   159	
   160	**Lemma 4.2 (monomials).** Let `p = k^d` and `(k_1, k_2, k_3) = (1, 2, −4)` with types F, F, G, so
   161	`w = (−1, −2^d, 4^d)`. Then
   162	
   163	    Φ_d = (6^d − 4^d − 2^d)(3^d + 2^d − 4^d − 1)(3^d + 1 − 4^d) + (5^d − 4^d − 1)(5^d − 4^d − 2^d + 1)(2^{d+1} − 4^d),
   164	
   165	`Φ_2 = Φ_3 = 0` and `Φ_d < 0` for every `d ≥ 4` (`Φ_4 = −672768`). The hypotheses of Lemma 4.1 hold for all
   166	`d ≥ 2`, for (1) (`k_12, k_13, k_23, k_123 = 3, −3, −2, −1`) and for (2) with `l = (1, 3, 7)`.
   167	
   168	*Proof.* `4 ≤ d ≤ 60`: exact integer evaluation (Part C). `d ≥ 30`: the first product is positive and
   169	`< 6^d·4^d·4^d = 96^d` (its last two factors are negative with absolute values `< 4^d`). In the second product,
   170	for `d ≥ 4` the first two factors are `≥ 5^d − 2·4^d > 0` and the last is `≤ −4^d/2`, so it is
   171	`≤ −100^d (1 − 2·0.8^d)^2 / 2`. Hence `Φ_d < 0` whenever `(1 − 2·0.8^d)^2 ≥ 2·0.96^d`; this holds at d = 30
   172	(checked exactly) and the left side increases and the right side decreases with d. Nondegeneracy:
   173	`P(κ_12) = 3^d − 2^d − 1 > 0`; `P(−κ_12) = 1 + 2^d + (−3)^d ≠ 0`; `P(κ_13) = 4^d − 1 + (−3)^d > 0`;
   174	`P(−κ_13) = 1 + 3^d − 4^d < 0`; `P(κ_23) = 4^d − 2^d + (−2)^d > 0`; `P(−κ_23) = 2^{d+1} − 4^d < 0`;
   175	`P(κ_123) = 4^d − 2^d − 1 + (−1)^d > 0`; `P(−κ_123) = 2 + 2^d − 4^d < 0` (all for `d ≥ 2`, `d ≥ 3` where
   176	needed); Part C rechecks them for `d ≤ 60`. ∎
   177	
   178	Part C also recomputes the full recursion for `d = 4, …, 9` for (1) and for (2) and confirms that the engine's
   179	`(2,1,1)` residual equals the closed form and is nonzero.
   180	
   181	**Lemma 4.3 (lower-order terms).** Let `deg p = d ≥ 4` with leading coefficient `c_d`. At the configuration
   182	`s·(1, 2, −4)` (and `l = (1, 3, 7)` for (2)), each `P(±κ_S)` and each factor of Φ is a polynomial in s of
   183	degree ≤ d whose `s^d` coefficient is `c_d` times the corresponding monomial value of Lemma 4.2. Hence
   184	`Φ(s)` has `s^{3d}` coefficient `c_d^3 Φ_d ≠ 0`, every hypothesis of Lemma 4.1 holds for all but finitely many
   185	s, and the 3ST fails. (Part D shows nonzero residuals for `k^5+k^2`, `k^4+k^3`, `k^6−3k^2+k`, `2k^5−k^3+k`,
   186	`k^7+k^5+k^3` at s = 1, 2, 5, 20.) ∎
   187	
   188	**Theorem 4.4 (classification in 𝒫).** For `P = D_t + p(D_x)`, `p(0) = 0`, `deg p ≥ 2`, the following are
   189	equivalent: (a) (1) passes the generic 3ST; (b) (2) passes it; (c) `deg p ≤ 3`.
   190	
   191	*Proof.* (c)⇒(b): C3. (b)⇒(a): C1. (a)⇒(c): if `deg p ≥ 4`, Lemmas 4.1–4.3 with `M = D_x` give an
   192	F F G configuration with nonzero residual; F- and G-type seeds exist for every p, and for odd p they are
   193	specialisations of the two-amplitude seeds. ∎ (Lemmas 4.1–4.3 with `M = D_y` show directly that (2) fails.)
   194	
   195	**Corollaries.**
   196	
   197	- *Li–Lou question (7).* `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` passes the generic 3ST iff
   198	  n ∈ {2, 3} (n = 1 is linear and degenerate). For n ≥ 4 this system and its (1+1)-dimensional counterpart
   199	  fail the generic test: the F F G pattern has no solution of the stated form. Some multi-soliton families
   200	  survive for every p — e.g. `F = 1 + Σ_i a_i e^{η_i}`, `G = 1` (all F type, a linear superposition) — and
   201	  families with constrained parameters or other ansätze are not excluded. So for n ≥ 4 the model is not
   202	  integrable in the sense of the generic Hirota multi-soliton property; the result says nothing about Lax or
   203	  symmetry integrability. This answers, in that sense and negatively, the question left open in Li & Lou
   204	  (2008), Section 4.
   205	- *(R-sys-3) holds in 𝒫* with `R(S) = Q(D_y) = D_y`: the hypothesis and the conclusion hold for exactly the
   206	  same P. The all-N statement (R-sys) in 𝒫 reduces to the open obligation in C6.
