     1	# phys-bilinear-higher-dim-integrability
     2	
     3	Book problem: 关于得到高维可积模型的一个新猜想 / "On a new conjecture to find higher-dimensional integrable
     4	models", Lou Senyue and Li Jinhua (Ningbo University), 《10000个科学难题·物理学卷》 (Science Press, 2009),
     5	book pp. 350–351 (PDF pp. 383–384).
     6	
     7	## Summary of the book problem
     8	
     9	Lou and Li conjecture a "holographic" route from (1+1)-dimensional to higher-dimensional integrable models in
    10	Hirota's bilinear formalism: if the bilinear system `P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0, Q(D_x)P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0` is
    11	integrable, then `P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0, R(S)P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0` may be integrable, where S is the Hirota
    12	operator of an extra (continuous, discrete, supersymmetric or q-deformed) variable and R is a polynomial,
    13	often of the same form as Q. The printed `D_c` in the second system is a typo for `D_t` (STATUS.md §1). The
    14	book asks how to prove the conjecture, how to determine R(S), and which extra conditions make it true if it is
    15	false. The cited source (Li & Lou, Chin. Phys. B 17, 747 (2008)) proves three examples by symmetry methods
    16	and leaves open whether `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` is integrable for other n. Its n = 3
    17	model coincides with a bilinear pair found by Hietarinta (1987); Peng et al. (2008) report Wronskian
    18	N-solitons and a Lax pair for a case of Hietarinta's pair, presumably this one (STATUS.md).
    19	
    20	## Formulations attacked
    21	
    22	"Integrable" is made checkable as Hirota's multi-soliton property (RESULTS.md §0):
    23	
    24	- **(R-sys)**, literal: with Q = D_x, R = D_y (any linear Q, R reduces to this), if the two-function system (1)
    25	  passes the generic N-soliton test (all one-soliton seeds, independent amplitudes in F and G), so does (2).
    26	  Studied in the class 𝒫: `P = D_t + p(D_x)`, `p(0) = 0`, `deg p ≥ 2`, which contains Li & Lou's family.
    27	- **(R-eq)**, single-function: the F = G reduction, `Q(D_x)P f·f = 0` integrable ⇒ `Q(D_y)P f·f = 0`
    28	  integrable (P, Q odd).
    29	
    30	Not covered: models whose integrability is invisible to exponential solitons (e.g. `P = D_x D_t`),
    31	supersymmetric and q-deformed S.
    32	
    33	## Verdict
    34	
    35	**PARTIAL** for the literal conjecture and **COUNTEREXAMPLE** for its single-function reading (in the Hirota
    36	multi-soliton sense of "integrable"). FRONTIER state: PROOF-OBLIGATION, NOVELTY-UNCLEAR.
    37	
    38	- In 𝒫 the literal conjecture holds at the three-soliton level: (1) passes the generic three-soliton test ⇔
    39	  (2) passes it ⇔ `deg p ≤ 3` (Theorem 4.4). In particular Li & Lou's system passes only for n = 2, 3; for
    40	  n ≥ 4 it fails the generic test (the F F G soliton pattern has no solution of Hirota form), although some
    41	  restricted families, e.g. linear superpositions with G = 1, always exist. The same classification holds
    42	  with the discrete operator `R(S) = sinh(D_n)` (Proposition 7.1).
    43	- The single-function reading is false: `D_x(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0` (Sawada–Kotera) is integrable, but
    44	  `D_y(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0` fails Hirota's three-soliton condition; the obstruction (proportional to `c5`) is
    45	  computed exactly and reproduces the constraint found by Wang et al. (2024).
    46	- So, at the three-soliton level and in 𝒫, the extra condition asked for by the book is: require (1) to pass
    47	  the test as a genuine two-component system (independent F and G amplitudes), not only on its F = G
    48	  reduction. Whether this suffices for all N is open.
    49	- Open: the N-soliton property for all N in the passing class. For odd cubic p a Pfaffian closed form for the
    50	  two-amplitude N-soliton solution is conjectured and matches the exact recursive solution for N ≤ 7 at
    51	  sample points (RESULTS.md C6); proving it for all N would settle the literal conjecture in 𝒫 for odd p.
    52	  P outside 𝒫 is open (an exploratory scan found no counterexample).
    53	
