     1	# Status and state of the art (as of 2026-10-14)
     2	
     3	Book problem: 关于得到高维可积模型的一个新猜想 (On a new conjecture to find higher-dimensional integrable
     4	models), 楼森岳 (Lou Senyue) and 李金花 (Li Jinhua), Ningbo University, in 《10000个科学难题·物理学卷》
     5	(Science Press, 2009), book pp. 350–351 (PDF pp. 383–384). Source text: `source/problem.zh.txt`; page images:
     6	`source/loulipage-383.png`, `source/loulipage-384.png`.
     7	
     8	## 1. What the book states
     9	
    10	If the (1+1)-dimensional bilinear system
    11	
    12	    (1)  P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0,   Q(D_x) P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0
    13	
    14	is integrable, then
    15	
    16	    (2)  P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0,   R(S) P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0
    17	
    18	"may be" (可能是) integrable. P and Q are polynomials, R is an undetermined polynomial ("in many cases R has
    19	the same functional form as Q"), and S may be the Hirota operator of an extra dimension, a supersymmetric,
    20	discrete or q-deformed Hirota operator. The book says that Ref. [6] (Li & Lou 2008) gives seven simple
    21	successful examples, all with R and Q of the same linear form and R(S) = D_y or R(S) = A (a supersymmetric
    22	bilinear operator). It asks: how to prove the conjecture, how to determine R(S), and, if it fails, which extra
    23	conditions make it hold.
    24	
    25	Typography. The page image (`source/loulipage-384.png`) prints the second equation of (2) as
    26	`R(S)P(D_x, D_c)F,G = 0`. Both primary statements of the same ansatz write `P(D_t, D_x)` in both equations
    27	(Li & Lou 2008, Eqs. (62)–(63); Wang, Lou & Qian 2010, Eqs. (4)–(5)), so `D_c` is a typo for `D_t`, and
    28	`F,G` denotes the bilinear pair F·G.
    29	
    30	Precision. The book's statement is a heuristic: it does not define "integrable" and does not say whether
    31	integrability of (1) is meant for general (F, G) or only on special reductions such as F = G. The precise
    32	readings used here are fixed in `RESULTS.md` §0.
    33	
    34	## 2. Primary sources on the conjecture
    35	
    75	## 4. Settled vs open (2026-10-14)
    76	
    77	Settled in the literature:
    78	
    79	- Positive examples: the three Li–Lou models and the Wang–Lou–Qian supersymmetric model are shown
    80	  "integrable" in the sense of infinitely many (generalized or Lie point) symmetries. Neither of these two
    81	  papers constructs general N-soliton solutions of the two-function systems.
    82	- The n = 3 model (iii) is, up to renaming t ↔ y, Hietarinta's mKdV-type pair (37) with a = 0, found in 1987
    83	  to pass his three-soliton test (for his A- and B-type solitons); Peng, Zhang, Gegenhasi & Liu (2008) give
    84	  Wronskian N-soliton solutions, a bilinear Bäcklund transformation and a Lax representation for "a
    85	  particular case" of Hietarinta's system (abstract; that this case is a = 0 is inferred, see §2). If so,
    86	  model (iii) is integrable in the Lax sense, independently of Li & Lou's symmetry argument. Neither Li & Lou
    87	  2008 nor Wang, Lou & Qian 2010 cite these works.
    88	- The single-function (F = G) version of the n = 5 case, `D_y(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0`, has three- and
    89	  four-soliton solutions only under wave-number constraints (Wang et al. 2024, by explicit computation). The
    90	  generic three-soliton failure is implied by their need for constraint (16) but not stated as a theorem,
    91	  and the paper does not connect it to the Li–Lou conjecture.
    92	
    93	Explicitly open in the primary sources:
    94	
    95	- Li & Lou 2008, Section 4: "Because of complicity of calculation, we have not yet found other integrable
    96	  systems from Eq. (7)" — i.e. whether `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` is integrable for any
    97	  n other than 2, 3 — and "Even if the ansatz is not correct, we believe that we can still obtain many new
    98	  integrable models from the ansatz!"
    99	- Wang, Lou & Qian 2010: "the conjecture should be checked further".
   100	
   101	No proof, general counterexample or class-level characterization of the conjecture itself was found
   102	(searches of the web, OpenAlex citations of Li & Lou 2008, arXiv). The closest class-level result is
   103	Hietarinta's 1987 mKdV-type search: according to his 1997 summary, among the five pairs found only (37) has
   104	the shape `A = R·B` with R linear (search space and soliton families as in that paper, not read in the
   105	original). An unsuccessful search does not establish that nothing else exists.
   106	
   107	## 5. Baseline to beat
   108	
   109	- Strongest positive evidence: three continuous-variable examples (`P = D_x D_t`, `D_t + D_x^2`,
   110	  `D_t + D_x^3`, each with Q = D_x and R = D_y) and one supersymmetric example, verified by symmetry analysis;
   111	  for `D_t + D_x^3` additionally a three-soliton test (Hietarinta 1987) and Wronskian N-solitons plus a Lax
   112	  pair (Peng et al. 2008).
   113	- Strongest negative evidence: the Wang et al. (2024) constrained-soliton computation for the F = G reduction
   114	  of n = 5, not framed as evidence against the conjecture.
   115	- No class of (P, Q) is known for which the conjecture itself is proved, and no rule for R(S) beyond R = Q.
